  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThis morning we'll be keeping a close eye on just how efficient \nradiational cooling will be under clear skies, light to calm winds, \nand dewpoints in the upper 40s to mid 50s. Right now, Alice is \nalready at a 0-3 dewpoint depression, highly indicative of fog \ndevelopment given the several hours of additional radiational \ncooling left. Patchy to areas of fog is expected over the inland \nCoastal Plains to Brush Country, but just how dense is uncertain. \nAny fog will be lifted by mid-morning with sunny skies and \ntemperatures reaching the mid to upper 70s as onshore flow \ngradually returns. \n\nA cutoff mid-level low currently off the west coast will shift \neastward and get ingested into the base of the main trough this \nweekend, positioning itself directly over South Texas Saturday. This \nwill allow for low to medium rain chances, with coverage shifting \nwest to east through the day in coordination with the PVA associated \nwith the trough as it moves eastward. Following the trough, surface \ntroughing will amplify over South Texas in advance of an approaching \ncold front Sunday night into Monday, putting us in the favorable \nwarm sector with above-normal moisture Saturday night through Sunday \n(PWATs 75th percentile or greater). Rain chances increase to a \nmedium to high (40-90%) Sunday ahead of the front pushing through, \nthen lower Sunday night into Monday morning behind it.  \n\nRainfall through the weekend looks to remain under 1.50\" for most of \nSouth Texas, ensembles are in good agreement with higher amounts in \nthe 90th percentile around 4.0\" over the Victoria Crossroads. Sunday \nin particular has an extreme forecast index of 0.7 over the Victoria \nCrossroads, signifying an unusual event in terms of rainfall \namounts. Therefore, there is a marginal risk of flash flooding in \neffect. Given the recent rainfall, soils are more saturated than \nnormal and can lead to more efficient runoff and ponding. \n\nLastly, there is increasing concern for minor coastal flooding \nSunday into early next week as swell periods increase. Southeasterly \nwinds strengthen Sunday ahead of the front, before shifting out of \nthe northeast in the wake, resulting in the Ekman transport \nmechanism aiding in wave runup. Consequently, the rip current risk \nwill also increase, causing more dangerous swimming conditions.",
